t  we're using  and then another form of isomers comes  from  the change of the fatty acyl tail  orientation which is called the sn  position  and this is where the fatty acyl tails  are the same but the connectivities are  different  and finally we can get lipid isomers  based on  changes of the double bond position so  here we have the double bond at the  ninth carbon versus the 11th carbon  so to deal with these isomers we use a  liquid chromatography ion mobility mass  spectrometry method to achieve  multi-dimensional separations and so we  use a reverse phase cc18 column to  separate the lipids by their  hydrophobicity we use ion mobility which  i'll talk about in a second and then we  use a qtof mass spectrometer in all ions  mode meaning that we collect our data  with  alternating frames of low and high  collision energy to get precursor and  fragment ions for the lipids  and so um hopefully everyone is pretty  comfortable with lcms but i'm just going  to give a brief explanation of  immobility spectrometry specif  specifically the type that we're using  in our lab for anyone who might be less  familiar with that technique  so we use an agilent 6560 which is a  drift tube iron mobility qtof instrument  the drift tube is filled with a buffer  gas such as nitrogen  and then ions pass through the drift  tube and those with the bigger size will  have more collisions with that buffer  gas and so they take longer to travel  through that drift tube  and so the output we get is going to be  drift time which is going to be  larger for bigger molecules  these drift time values can easily be  converted using what is called a single  field calibration to collision cross  sections or ccs values  which describe the surface area of the  molecule and they are molecular  descriptors so they can be compared  across labs and compared to database  values  and so here the more extended lipid is  going to have a higher drift time and  therefore a higher ccs value  and so while there are many benefits to  using ion mobility there are also  challenges and so the multi-dimensional  data that we get is very large in  complexity and file size  and manual assignment can be time  consuming and can only be done for a few  targets with small data sets  and so here we have an example of what  that complex data might look like and so  we have a base peak chromatogram which  actually looks pretty clean compared to  a lot of our lipid samples  and if we want to take a closer look at  two of the peaks here we can see the  abundance maps which show the  drift time versus m over z  with the abundance being shown as a heat  map where red is going to be on most and  most abundant and then  blue is just going to be essentially  noise and so as you can see  each one of these peaks has a lot of  features so  there's really a lot to tease out in  this data  um and we're also only looking at the  low energy or precursor scans here and  so this can get even more complex when  we start looking at those high energy  fragmentation scans as well  and so to analyze this complex  liponomics data there are currently two  main options which we like to say is  sort of like a choose your fighter match  between the manual labor and the black  box  so the black box takes your data  compares it to database information  and gives you a list of lipids usually  with some sort of rank or confidence  score associated with each  identification  so the strength for this method is that  it's extremely fast it's good for large  data sets and it does a deep search  through extensive databases  um the weaknesses of this method are  that there's little to no validation for  the identifications  lipids are often often over annotated  meaning  someone may report the sn and double  bond position of a lipid when they may  only have data to support the fatty acyl  composition  and then you may have to sacrifice some  of that precious multi-dimensional data  that  you spent so long trying to collect such  as retention time and ccs because some  databases won't use those values  and then the search may not be sample or  species specific  and on the other hand the manual labor  is where we started when i joined the  lab and that requires that you manually  determine  all the characteristics for your little  bit of interest so  you have to find um the formula  calculate the mo for z with the adducts  you're interested in  find the fragmentation information look  at another database for the ccs value so  it can be very time consuming just to  collect  all of that information and then you  have to go dig through your data  to find those lipids and so the  strengths of this method are that you  get very confident annotations you can  utilize all that um  multi-dimensional data  and you can prioritize biologically  relevant lipids meaning those that you  expect to see in your species and sample  type or those that you're interested in  given the specific conditions you're  looking at but the weaknesses of this  method are that it's extremely time  consuming and it's not sustainable for  large data sets or deep searches  and so what we really wanted to do was  take all these strengths and create our  own method to analyze our lipid data and  so we decided that skyline was the ideal  software to do that with  when we started using skyline for our  lipid data analysis we started out with  a really simple flow um about as simple  as you can get so skyline for those who  aren't familiar with it is a targeted  data analysis software but it still  works well for untargeted data as long  as you have a list of molecules or m or  z values of interest to start with  and so our initial workflow started with  just a simple list of lipids and their m  over z values  importing and analyzing the data and  then exporting  a little bit of information  but since then  we've worked with the skyline team to  add some new small molecule capabilities  and then we've also taken advantage of a  lot of their existing features and we've  come up with a proposed workflow for  those who are interested in using  skyline for lipid analysis  where everything in yellow is new  compared to our initial workflow  and so now our target list um has lipids  that have formulas meaning we can look  at the isotope distributions we have  multiple adducts fragments even some  neutral losses our ccs values and  normalized retention times  and now when we import our data we're  filtering in multiple dimensions instead  of just by m over z  um and if we're using internal standards  we can also do some quantitation and  then we export a lot more information  and so some of the features that i'll  talk more about  in the rest of the presentation are  spectral libraries irt retention time  and drift time filtering  so what we've been doing is taking our  data from many different sample types  and developing spectral libraries in  skyline  where each lipid has information on its  m over z value its fragments  its ccs and its retention time  and so the strengths of this method are  that it is sample specific  multi-dimensional the analyses are rapid  but you can manually validate your  results  it's good for large data sets and target  lists  anyone who imports the spectral library  can prioritize the lipids that they're  interested in and can also customize the  target  list for their use  the libraries are generated from deep  searches with confident and appropriate  annotations meaning they're not over  annotated  and skyline offers straightforward  visualization and quantitation tools and  of course is vendor independent and free  and so our resulting libraries contain  hundreds of lipids from diverse classes  from these five lipid categories out of  the eight defined by lipid maps that i  mentioned earlier  and the other categories either  aren't present in the samples that we've  looked at so far or they just require  more specialized extractions  so to give you an example of what a  single library might be comprised of  here we have a sample specific library  which has over 750 features from both  positive and negative mode  and this boils down to about 500 unique  lipids when you consolidate any lipids  that have multiple adducts or that were  annotating in both ionization modes  and then when we plot this uh  library all the lipids here  by their collision cross sections and m  over z values we get these really nice  trend lines for each lipid class and so  in positive mode you can even see trends  within the trend lines and that  corresponds to  lipids that have the same total number  of carbons but varying degrees of  unsaturation  and these trend lines can really help us  validate our lipid annotations um by  verifying that we don't have any  outliers which would point to  that lipid maybe belonging to a  different class and so this can be  helpful when we're looking at those  isomers that arise from those class or  head group changes that i talked about  earlier  and then we can also compare our  experimentally determined ccs values  with those present in the ccs compendium  out of john mclean's lab at vanderbilt  um for more confidence in our  annotations and so all the ccs values  for the lipids in our current libraries  um that have a compendium match are less  than two percent different than that uh  database value which is  right where we'd want to be  so while our user workflow is really  straightforward and only requires some  manual work for validations uh we put a  lot of work into creating these  libraries so i wanted to just take a  moment to go over that process for those  who may want to create their own  libraries for a unique sample type or  maybe even a different set of molecules  so first we use the lipid creator tool  in skyline to generate transition lists  with thousands of lipids  and this is another free tool  it's actually available as a plug-in for  skyline to interface directly with the  software and i highly recommend it for  anyone who is doing lipid work  we then search for those lipids in our  data and find candidate peaks based on  their mass measurement accuracy  the coalution of adducts and the  anticipated retention times given the  lipid subclass  then we use the skyline eye mobility  predictor tool which is used to  calculate ccs values of the candidate  lipids  which we can do with our single field  calibrated data to convert any feature  drift times to ccs values  and then lipids are annotated based on  their drift timeline fragments and their  ccs values  and then for each library we create a  new irt calculator and calibrate it to  contain normalized retention times for  all the lipids that are in our library  and i'll go into some more detail on  that in a moment  and finally we create and export our  spectral libraries and we plan to  share them on skylines online data  repository panorama where anyone can  access and download them  so like i mentioned earlier we use  skyline's index retention time or irt  calculator for retention time  predictions and normalizations and so to  walk you through how we use this tool um  we start by assigning a set of about 20  reference lipids that span our lc  gradient  and then skyline will assign them a  relative irt value from 0 to 100  and so we use  endogenous lipids as our reference  points to make sure that anyone can use  it but  many people will use internal standards  for this  and so we have plans to actually create  secondary calculators for our different  libraries using the  new avanti ultimate splash 1 mix which  is a mix of  about 70 lipids from different classes  and so we can use that as a set of more  standardized reference points  and so once we calibrate the calculator  um we can add the rest of the lipids in  our library so that each one is now  assigned a unique irt value  and skyline recently added the option to  use  in addition to linear regressions either  logarithmic or lowest regressions to set  the irt values and so we've actually  found that using a lowest regression has  worked pretty well for us when we are  looking at data from  lc methods that have different gradient  times  because linear regressions just don't  always capture the differences where  some uh gradient regions may be more  compressed than others when you lower  that  time  and so both of these two steps are  something that we handle while we're  generating the library and then someone  who's using it would go in and use the  irt values to now predict the retention  times  of all the lipids in a new data set  regardless of the gradient time they're  using or any retention time shifts  so a good example of the  prediction potential of the irt  calculator is with lysolipids which are  lipids of one fatty acell chain cleaved  off and so  the sn1 and sn2 lysol lipids actually  generally do separate by lc but they  have identical fragments and then  um usually the ccs values are are too  close to distinguish them and so it's  important for us to  know which peak is for which lipid  and so here  skyline is correctly predicting that the  sn2  lysolipid alludes first  and then using that auto calculate  regression feature skyline will actually  adjust for any retention time shifts  between samples um as you can see here  with the two different predictions at  8.3 minutes versus 8.4 minutes when that  peak shifts just a little bit between  these two samples  and then we can see that the same thing  holds true for the sn1 lysolipid which  is going to be that second alluding peak  here at 8.7 or 8.8 minutes  and then in addition to predicting  predicting retention times the irt  calculator is really useful for  standardizing retention times and so  here we're looking at the retention time  of a single pe  across four different experiments and  this is in  avonte's brain total lipid extract which  is something that we use as a qc  standard in all of our lipidomics  experiments just to keep track of our  instrument performance and our lcd  performance over time  and so this first set of three  measurements was taken um here at nc  state on our current instrument and then  the rest were taken  at pnnl on a different instrument and a  couple different columns and so you can  see that this retention time can vary  pretty significantly anywhere from about  20 to close to 23 minutes  but when we convert to the irt scale  which is just an arbitrary 0 to 100  scale we can see that these values  become much more comparable and  standardized all at about 79.  so moving on from irt another feature  of our workflow is spectral library  matching where you can view the library  match just directly in that skyline  window  next to your data and you can make just  direct comparisons to see  how they match up and so here we have an  ms2 spectrum for a ceramide that we  detected in one data set  and then here's the library match  underneath  and then as you can see on the right  side of the slide these relative peak  area profiles match up pretty well so we  can be confident  in our detection of the ceramide in this  experimental data set  and then another aspect of the libraries  that i mentioned earlier is that each  lipid has a stored ccs value  which can be used for drift time  filtering if you're using iron mobility  but can easily be ignored if not  and so i mentioned that  our ccs values are calculated from drift  times using a single field calibration  and when your data file has been  calibrated and the vendor software which  we do in our agilent software  skyline can actually back calculate  drift time from ccs and so  we set a resolving power tolerance in  skyline just based on our instrument  and then skyline uses  both that ccs the calibration and then  that tolerance to determine the window  of drift times that contains the signal  that we want to extract and you can see  that with the  horizontal purple boxes here  and so in both of these examples um  these other signals would interfere with  our lipid of interest signal and  probably contribute to the extracted  intensities that we were getting so we  can  try to filter them out based on their  drift times to get a better idea of  the true abundance of the lipid that  we're actually interested in  and so this is a very important function  especially with lots of lipid isomers  and lipids that differ just by one  double bond  but i'd argue that drift time filtering  can be even more valuable when it comes  to lipid fragments especially in  negative mode  so as you can see with this pe lipid the  fragment signals for these two fatty  acyl chains 18 0 and 24  which are the teal and orange  chromatogram traces here  uh pretty much dominate the extracted  ion chromatogram um and this is due to  the fact that most of the lipids in our  sample with these two really common  fatty easel chains  will share these same fragments and our  dia method does not do any precursor  isolation when we're fragmenting  so the same fragment can be seen along  really wide ranges of our gradient  and so in some cases these fragments can  completely drown out the precursor  and this makes it difficult to annotate  lipids and can throw off the relative  intensities of our spectral library  match  but if we use drift time filtering uh we  can filter out all this excess signal  from these shared fragments  and our chromatogram looks much more  clear when only the signal that's  contributing um to our extracted  intensity  are at that correct drift time of our  little bit of interest so you can see at  the bottom here at that fatty acyl 18-0  peak  um that we obviously had another lipid  at that same retention time at a much  higher drift time that was contributing  to our fragment signal  and so one last capability that i'd like  to point out which can often get  overlooked in lipidomics is the utility  of analyzing multiple adducts for a  single lipid and so in positive mode pc  lipids form really highly abundant  protonated species um but collision  induced dissociation which is the  fragmentation  cell that we're using produces really  low neutral loss  low in abundance neutral loss fragments  and so in this example which is another  lysolipid  um any pair of peaks here could be the  correct pair if we were just looking at  the protonated species because those  fragments are so low in abundance we  wouldn't really be able to tell them  apart  but when we add in the sodium species  which is this  um brown  set of peaks we can see really clearly  see which pair of peaks is the correct  pair so adding in that second adduct  really helps us filter some of these  candidate peaks  and then another example  of where adding the sodium species can  be helpful is in assigning the fatty  acid chain composition of lipids  and so for this example lipid um which  is pretty representative of most pcs  we can barely see the neutral loss  fragments um  that would help us in the annotation of  this lipid  even though it has a very high signal  and so we wouldn't really be able to  call it a pc 18 124 because we wouldn't  be sure  of those fatty acyl chains  and so here the peak area of the  protonated precursor is an order of  magnitude greater than that of the  sodium heated species  but the sodiated species offers a lot  more fragments to help in that fatty  acyl assignment and these fragments make  up more of the total peak area with  about 10 percent as opposed to  about a quarter of a percent  and so before i wrap things up i just  wanted to give some examples of  different applications that our lab is  working on um that are using using these  libraries  so for example um i'm interested in  assessing lipid changes in various  systems following environmental  exposures  um so such as the ones on the top here  exposures to pfas or exposures to  combustion products in smoke  and so from these more environmental to  some more life sciences applications  these lipid libraries are really  facilitating a lot of the exciting  research that's happening in our lab and  hopefully in some other labs as soon as  we make them publicly available  so in the meantime if you're interested  in using skyline to process  any lipidomics or other small molecule  ion mobility data or if you just want to  learn more about creating and using  spectral libraries in skyline we worked  with the skyline team to create a  step-by-step tutorial for this type of  data analysis  it's a hands-on tutorial with some of  our fruit fly  lipid extract data  and it should be posted on the skyline  website any day now  and so to wrap up  just a few take-home points would be  that skyline allows rapid and confident  analysis of complex multi-dimensional  data which in our case is going to be lc  ims  msms  and we're working to develop some sample  specific spectral libraries containing m  over z retention time and ccs data for  hundreds of lipids  and then some of the key features of  skyline software including irt spectral  library matching and drift time  filtering  as well as seeing those adduct illusion  profiles can really allow for some  confident lipid annotations  and i'd also like to mention here that  skyline has lots of other features  outside of what i've discussed today and  they're always adding in some new  functionalities to their  software to make it useful for really  any data analysis workflow so i highly  recommend checking it out and seeing how  it can fit into your data analysis  workflow  and so with that i would like to thank  the baker lab  members our funding sources specifically  the nih molecular biotechnology training  program  and the whole team at skyline especially  those who have worked on the tools that  i talked about today or have worked on  the small molecule support  and of course thank you to the agilent  team for inviting me to speak today and  to you all for listening  and with that i'm happy to take any  questions  really for that incredible presentation  we will now begin the live q a portion  of our presentation again check out the  left side of the q a box and ask a  question so we hope you will do that we  already have some great questions coming  in  our first question what are some other  ways skyline can be incorporated into  lipidomics data analysis workflows  yeah um that's a great question and i  think um  it's important to point out that what i  was talking about in this presentation  is outside of the norm for what people  are using skyline for so  skyline like i said in the presentation  is a targeted software  um and so certainly um any targeted  lipidomics data if you're doing srm mrn  prm type methods um that is really what  skyline is built for and it's a it's a  big workforce um for for targeted data  and so that's certainly a workflow where  it's  really easily incorporated even before  you start running  any samples you can use it for your  collision energy optimization  creating methods and so  that's definitely one  place where it fits in well and then  another one would be  with validating any results that you do  get from any database searches so if  you're using a software  like lipid annotator or  lipid blast one of the ones that are  vendor neutral then you can always sort  of  rework the spreadsheet that you get at  the end there and  um  you can um  just throw that into skyline and then  just validate the  the peaks that you are seeing in the  database  thank you so much and you know what the  interferences could be on the drift time  filtering slide  yeah um  so  on that side um those data  files were collected in negative mode  and so those were both pe lipids and i  believe those interferences were one  dalton um  lower than  the lipids that we were looking at so i  would say that those were likely  sm lipids those fingo myelins because  those are sort of in that same retention  time window as the pe lipids and just  one dalton away  from the pe if they were two dots away i  would say that that would be  a loss of a double bond which is pretty  common in lipidomics analyses  thank you so much and thank you to our  audience members for these great  questions how much do you manually  validate the peak  picking in skyline kaylee  yeah that's another great question um  and i would say that i try to manually  validate um all the peaks that skyline  has picked even if it's just a really  quick run through to check that the  retention times match up and that the  um peak area profiles match up  um i think that  um  i guess i'll try to quote um will  thompson from duke he said that um the  same the same things are happening um  with any software that you're using  where you might get peaks that are  picked improperly but skyline just gives  you the opportunity to fix it and so i  definitely take advantage of that  opportunity um and fix any of the  the peak picking um  boundaries that kind of go awry  thank you so much we have time for one  more question what is your typical  lcms runtime  um so our typical  run time um we use a 38 minute gradient  which is pretty long and i'll just wrap  in another question that i see in the  chat with that um  which is that we've gone down to about  19 minutes um  and that is kind of where we'll start  losing some of the separations that we  would get and so really anywhere in that  20 to close to 40 minute range is where  we're comfortable running our lc methods  thank you kaylee for this informative  presentation would you like to provide  the audience with some closing remarks  before we go today  um i'll just say again thank you so much  to the agilent team for inviting me to  speak here today and thank you all for  for tuning in  and thank you again and as a reminder to  our audience questions we did not have  time for today and those  that are  um and those that were not  addressed during the on demand period  will be addressed  via your email you provided at the time  of registration and i want to thank our  speaker again kaylee and to everyone who  participated in the presentation we hope  you will check out the exhibit hall and  enjoy the rest of the agilent event  take care stay healthy bye bye for now
 

